{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>On four pillars of shittim.</b> These pillars stood in the open space of the breadth of the Mishkan, because there is no bother or crowding there, since no one enters or goes out there except the High Priest four times on a single day in the year. But at the entrance of the Mishkan there were five pillars: two of them, one opposite the southern wall and one opposite the northern wall, and the third opposite the open space of the interior of the Mishkan — because people enter and go out there constantly, and if there were more than three pillars there, they would obstruct and crowd those entering and going out. And one may say the reason there were only four pillars for the parokhet is that it was placed between the two walls of the Mishkan, and they could rest the crossbar on which the parokhet hangs on top of the two walls of the Mishkan to the north and to the south. But for the screen, which was placed under the open sky and had no walls at its sides as the parokhet had, five pillars were required. But this is not reasonable, for if so, what purpose would the pillars serve, which Rashi and Onkelos say were fixed in them etc.? Rather, the first interpretation is primary."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:42.065582027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}